From 51b3161f12af9255c6ae1d4734556d8143c7d28a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Eriksen <10115530+NeskireDK@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 20:16:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Record the content kind of subscription feed entries
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Invidious has no way to tell a Short from a long-form upload in a feed.
`ChannelVideo#to_json` reports `"type": "shortVideo"` for every row —
legacy naming for "abbreviated video object" — and `VideoType` exists
only on the full watch-page object, with no Short member. Length cannot
stand in: `channel_videos.length_seconds` is only filled for entries that
also appeared in the channel's Videos tab, so Shorts and stream VODs both
land as 0. On one 75-channel instance that was 531 of 1586 rows (34%).

YouTube publishes the missing signal as a per-channel uploads playlist
per kind, addressed by replacing the "UC" of the channel ID: UULF
long-form, UUSH Shorts, UULV live, each served by
/feeds/videos.xml?playlist_id=. Measured over those 75 channels, 15
newest entries each: 1114 long-form, 679 Shorts, 299 live, and one Short
leaking into a UULF feed.

ClassifyChannelVideosJob labels `kind` from those feeds in two passes —
the Shorts and live windows per channel, then a capped `HEAD /shorts/<id>`
probe (200 = Short, 303 = not) for rows older than a 15-entry window.
`feed_kinds` restricts the subscription feed through a predicate in each
user's materialized view, and ChannelVideo now reports the real type.

Deliberately a separate job rather than an edit to `fetch_channel`, and
`kind` is absent from the insert's `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` set, so a
channel refresh cannot overwrite a label. Views are rebuilt beside the
live one and swapped in, never dropped first.

Unclassified entries are always shown and an empty `feed_kinds` admits
everything, so neither a late job nor a bad value can blank a feed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
